1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,982
By the end of this, you will know how to clean and oil your skate bearings properly, in about twenty minutes.

2
00:00:06,982 --> 00:00:14,797
You will also know which solvents are safe, which ones quietly ruin a bearing, and when to stop cleaning and replace them.

3
00:00:14,797 --> 00:00:17,744
Bearing care is easy and costs almost nothing.

4
00:00:17,744 --> 00:00:21,524
It is also the highest return piece of skate care there is.

5
00:00:21,524 --> 00:00:29,083
Yet most skaters never do it, or do it badly enough that they would have been better off leaving their bearings alone.

6
00:00:29,083 --> 00:00:37,154
The principles are the same for steel or ceramic, inline or quad, so this routine keeps whatever you own rolling like day one.

7
00:00:37,854 --> 00:00:39,876
A bearing is a simple machine.

8
00:00:39,876 --> 00:00:41,965
Balls run between two raceways.

9
00:00:41,965 --> 00:00:46,143
A cage holds them in place, and seals protect the whole thing.

10
00:00:46,143 --> 00:00:48,502
Look at the two raceways on screen.

11
00:00:48,502 --> 00:00:52,545
When the raceway is clean and lightly oiled, the ball rolls.

12
00:00:52,545 --> 00:00:58,408
When a hard particle gets onto that raceway, the ball starts to skid and grind instead.

13
00:00:58,408 --> 00:01:02,991
Every one of those tiny skids is speed you paid for and did not get.

14
00:01:02,991 --> 00:01:07,843
Worse, a particle scores the surface, and that score collects more dirt.

15
00:01:07,843 --> 00:01:10,741
So a dirty bearing does not just stay slow.

16
00:01:10,741 --> 00:01:12,358
It keeps getting slower.

17
00:01:13,058 --> 00:01:17,390
Three things cause the decline, and they make each other worse.

18
00:01:17,390 --> 00:01:18,834
First, contamination.

19
00:01:18,834 --> 00:01:23,303
Dust, grit and road film work their way past the seals over time.

20
00:01:23,303 --> 00:01:24,266
Second, water.

21
00:01:24,266 --> 00:01:25,985
Water is worse than dirt.

22
00:01:25,985 --> 00:01:32,448
It flushes the lubricant out and leaves bare steel, and steel plus water plus time means rust.

23
00:01:32,448 --> 00:01:38,843
One skate through a puddle can undo a season of care if you do not deal with it that evening.

24
00:01:38,843 --> 00:01:40,906
Third, old or wrong lubricant.

25
00:01:40,906 --> 00:01:46,269
Oil breaks down and turns into a paste that slows you more than the dirt does.

26
00:01:46,269 --> 00:01:51,082
And a heavy garage oil in a speed bearing is like running in wet sand.

27
00:01:51,782 --> 00:01:55,023
Set aside twenty minutes and work in good light.

28
00:01:55,023 --> 00:01:55,630
Step one.

29
00:01:55,630 --> 00:02:01,706
Take the wheels off and push the bearings out with a bearing puller or the end of an axle.

30
00:02:01,706 --> 00:02:06,634
Never lever against the inner race with anything sharp, and never hammer.

31
00:02:06,634 --> 00:02:14,668
Keep them in order, because if one position keeps wearing out bearings, that usually points to a frame or axle problem.

32
00:02:14,668 --> 00:02:15,275
Step two.

33
00:02:15,275 --> 00:02:20,271
If your seals come out, ease them out from the inner edge with a fine pin.

34
00:02:20,271 --> 00:02:23,916
Go gently, because a bent seal never sits right again.

35
00:02:23,916 --> 00:02:24,659
Step three.

36
00:02:24,659 --> 00:02:32,422
Agitate the bearings in solvent in a sealed jar, then move them to a second jar of clean solvent for a final rinse.

37
00:02:32,422 --> 00:02:35,258
That second rinse is the step people skip.

38
00:02:35,258 --> 00:02:37,351
Then spin each bearing by hand.

39
00:02:37,351 --> 00:02:40,456
Never blast a dry bearing with compressed air.

40
00:02:40,456 --> 00:02:44,371
That party trick has ruined more bearings than any puddle.

41
00:02:45,072 --> 00:02:49,780
Step four is drying, and it decides whether your work was worth it.

42
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:55,895
Solvent left inside thins the new oil, and water left inside starts rust straight away.

43
00:02:55,895 --> 00:02:59,620
Lay the bearings on a clean cloth and give them time.

44
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:00,533
Warmth helps.

45
00:03:00,533 --> 00:03:02,009
Strong heat does not.

46
00:03:02,009 --> 00:03:04,820
Step five is oil, and here less is more.

47
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:09,529
Use one drop per bearing, then spin it slowly by hand to spread it.

48
00:03:09,529 --> 00:03:11,356
On screen you can see why.

49
00:03:11,356 --> 00:03:14,378
One drop finds the raceway and rolls clean.

50
00:03:14,378 --> 00:03:17,330
A flooded bearing collects dust and drags.

51
00:03:17,330 --> 00:03:17,963
Step six.

52
00:03:17,963 --> 00:03:22,320
Press the seals back in evenly until they click and sit flush.

53
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:23,515
Refit the wheels.

54
00:03:23,515 --> 00:03:31,176
If a wheel spun well on the bench but binds once bolted in, your axle is too tight or your spacers are wrong.

55
00:03:31,876 --> 00:03:34,411
A good bearing cleaner has two jobs.

56
00:03:34,411 --> 00:03:40,046
It must dissolve old oil and carry grit away, and then it must leave completely.

57
00:03:40,046 --> 00:03:41,314
Four options pass.

58
00:03:41,314 --> 00:03:44,624
A dedicated bearing cleaner is the easy answer.

59
00:03:44,624 --> 00:03:48,146
A citrus based degreaser is gentle on nylon cages.

60
00:03:48,146 --> 00:03:53,780
White spirit is strongest on thick old grease, but it needs a patient, full dry.

61
00:03:53,780 --> 00:04:01,317
And ninety nine percent isopropyl alcohol flashes off fast and leaves nothing, so it suits a quick refresh.

62
00:04:01,317 --> 00:04:03,148
Now the list to keep away.

63
00:04:03,148 --> 00:04:09,065
Acetone, which is in most nail polish remover, attacks nylon cages and rubber seals.

64
00:04:09,065 --> 00:04:12,375
Petrol, diesel and kerosene leave an oily film.

65
00:04:12,375 --> 00:04:14,911
Brake cleaner strips the steel bare.

66
00:04:14,911 --> 00:04:18,644
And water, soap and dishwashers are the worst of all.

67
00:04:19,343 --> 00:04:21,184
Why is acetone such a trap?

68
00:04:21,184 --> 00:04:23,434
Because it is a superb degreaser.

69
00:04:23,434 --> 00:04:29,162
A cage bathed in acetone can look perfect and then crack under load two weeks later.

70
00:04:29,162 --> 00:04:36,457
Water is the worst because it reaches every surface inside a bearing and then refuses to leave any of them.

71
00:04:36,457 --> 00:04:39,184
A few habits make the whole job cleaner.

72
00:04:39,184 --> 00:04:44,639
Use two sealed, labelled jars, so you never top up the rinse with dirty solvent.

73
00:04:44,639 --> 00:04:47,025
Wear gloves and work in moving air.

74
00:04:47,025 --> 00:04:55,821
Keep the solvent and the oil well apart on the bench, because oil with a splash of solvent in it is no longer the oil you bought.

75
00:04:55,821 --> 00:05:04,889
Let the jars stand so the grit settles, reuse the clean solvent from the top, and dispose of the sludge properly, never down a drain.

76
00:05:05,589 --> 00:05:07,575
Not every bearing comes apart.

77
00:05:07,575 --> 00:05:12,144
Look at one in good light and work out which of three types you have.

78
00:05:12,144 --> 00:05:17,640
A rubber seal that lifts out is the easy case, and you get the full six step clean.

79
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:19,693
A metal shield needs judgement.

80
00:05:19,693 --> 00:05:23,070
Many have a thin retaining ring you can lift first.

81
00:05:23,070 --> 00:05:26,447
But if the shield resists and starts to bend, stop.

82
00:05:26,447 --> 00:05:29,096
A bent shield rubs on the balls forever.

83
00:05:29,096 --> 00:05:32,076
Bearings you cannot open get a flush instead.

84
00:05:32,076 --> 00:05:39,094
Agitate them longer, change to fresh solvent, agitate again, and dry them for longer than feels necessary.

85
00:05:39,094 --> 00:05:43,796
Then put a drop of oil at the gap by the inner race and turn it slowly.

86
00:05:43,796 --> 00:05:48,166
A flush never reaches what a full clean does, so do it more often.

87
00:05:48,866 --> 00:05:50,892
How often should you do this?

88
00:05:50,892 --> 00:05:52,987
It depends on where you skate.

89
00:05:52,987 --> 00:05:57,946
On a clean, dry indoor track, every couple of months is usually plenty.

90
00:05:57,946 --> 00:06:03,604
On outdoor roads and mixed surfaces, clean monthly, or whenever the roll changes.

91
00:06:03,604 --> 00:06:09,331
Anywhere dusty, gritty or humid, clean more often and check after every hard week.

92
00:06:09,331 --> 00:06:14,151
After any contact with water, clean them that evening, no exceptions.

93
00:06:14,151 --> 00:06:17,503
A quick drop of oil between full cleans is fine.

94
00:06:17,503 --> 00:06:20,297
And ceramic bearings need this care too.

95
00:06:20,297 --> 00:06:28,749
The balls are harder than steel, but the races are still steel, the cage is still nylon, and the seals still let dirt in.

96
00:06:28,749 --> 00:06:36,362
A dirty ceramic bearing can even damage its own races faster, so clean ceramic on the same schedule as steel.

97
00:06:37,062 --> 00:06:41,819
Cleaning extends a bearing's life, but it does not make it last forever.

98
00:06:41,819 --> 00:06:46,641
Retire a bearing if you see rust or pitting that survives a proper clean.

99
00:06:46,641 --> 00:06:53,313
Retire it if it still feels gritty or notchy when you turn it slowly, even after cleaning and oiling.

100
00:06:53,313 --> 00:07:01,439
Retire it if the inner and outer race rock against each other, if the cage is damaged, or if a seal will not seat any more.

101
00:07:01,439 --> 00:07:05,534
A damaged cage can fail suddenly and take the bearing with it.

102
00:07:05,534 --> 00:07:10,753
If only one bearing in a wheel is bad, replace the pair in that wheel at least.

103
00:07:10,753 --> 00:07:13,924
And brand new bearings usually need no cleaning.

104
00:07:13,924 --> 00:07:19,804
They come with the right lubricant inside, so adding oil on top just makes a heavier mix.

105
00:07:20,504 --> 00:07:21,975
A few quick answers.

106
00:07:21,975 --> 00:07:24,475
Can you use WD40 or household oil?

107
00:07:24,475 --> 00:07:25,505
Please do not.

108
00:07:25,505 --> 00:07:32,640
WD40 is a water displacer, not a lubricant, and household oils are too heavy for a speed bearing.

109
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:35,508
Your bearings sound fine but feel slow?

110
00:07:35,508 --> 00:07:36,832
Clean them anyway.

111
00:07:36,832 --> 00:07:42,496
Thick old oil can drag without making noise, because noise is a late symptom.

112
00:07:42,496 --> 00:07:44,335
How long should they dry?

113
00:07:44,335 --> 00:07:49,851
Until there is no smell of solvent and no damp anywhere, which means hours.

114
00:07:49,851 --> 00:07:51,837
A fan helps more than heat.

115
00:07:51,837 --> 00:08:00,222
And oil them the same day you clean them, because a freshly cleaned bearing is bare steel that can rust overnight.

116
00:08:00,922 --> 00:08:04,554
If you take one thing from this, make it the spin test.

117
00:08:04,554 --> 00:08:07,196
Every week, pick up a wheel and spin it.

118
00:08:07,196 --> 00:08:10,036
Listen, feel, and compare it to the others.

119
00:08:10,036 --> 00:08:15,385
If it sounds gritty or stops sooner than it used to, it is telling you something.

120
00:08:15,385 --> 00:08:23,046
That ten second habit catches bearing problems at the stage where a clean and one drop of oil fixes them completely.

121
00:08:23,046 --> 00:08:28,594
The full guide, with every solvent and seal type explained, is on the LiveLica blog.

122
00:08:28,594 --> 00:08:30,641
The link is in the description.
